Tim Cambrant <tim@cambrant.com> deserves a cookie for saying:
> I reach quite far into the gnomish mines almost every game, and usually die
> on XPLVL 6 or something like that, varying both upwards and downwards of
> course.

XL:6 isn't high enough for most classes to deal with the mines.  V and B
may be fine, but other classes will have, what, 40-45 HP, in general?
And likely a high AC.  If you're not gnomish, don't bet on surviving.  

> During the month of me playing valkyrie/wizard (i generally survive longer
> by using wizards for some reason), I got to sokoban once, but that was
> because I went for sokoban before the mines. I also reached the end of the
> mines _once_. This seems strange to me, and I'm starting to see a pattern in
> my high score-list. All characters 1-15 on the list died in either the
> gnomish mines or in sokoban.

Go to Sokoban first, unless there are extenuating circumstances.  The
mines are tough, but the only difficult level you need to go through to
get to Sokoban is the Oracle.  Once you're in Sokoban, you get plenty of
wands, rings, and food, plus the prize.  Then go back up to the mines
and you'll likely be around XL:9 or 10 and far better equipped.

Once you're in the mines, go down to the town, check the altar, collect
equipment, and leave.  Don't bother going down further unless you can
deal with polytraps, poison, dark levels, and heavy damage.  Go back up
to the main dungeon and move down that way.

> Well, long story, but here's my question. Why do I always die so
> early? I play very careful and have a quite large arsenal of spells
> and armor when I die, but it's always because of stupid things, such
> as a big monster killing me while I'm trying to pray or cast healing
> on myself.

You can only be killed while praying if the prayer didn't work.  Pay
more attention to your prayer timeout.  As for healing, you don't want
to get into a situation where you need to cast a healing spell on
yourself.  If you have 50 hp and you figure you'll heal when you're down
to 10, don't wait that long.  Run away when you're at 30.  

> The E-word helps sometimes, but it's easy to forget about.  How long
> did it take before you started to think of the mines as "easy"?

Depends.  I still don't think the mines are easy for a low-level
character.  

